 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Performance:** A single geometry unit can generate 26 to 36 million transformed locations per second using true 3D resampling [S1].
-*   **Memory Bandwidth:** Achieves up to 640 MB/s through conflict-free access of 8 neighbors, Fast Page Mode, and alternate bank read-out [S1].
-*   **Complexity:** The hardware offloads the 80% of computation time typically spent on geometry operations in software [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** The maximal resampled dataset size is limited to $512^3$ by the gradient buffer size [S1]. Original dataset size is constrained by the available memory technology (4 Mbit ORAMs vs 16 Mbit DRAMs) [S1].
-*   **Precision:** All calculations (density and gradient) are performed with 16-bit precision [S1].
+*   **Throughput:** A single geometry unit can generate 26 to 36 million transformed locations per second using true 3D resampling [S1].
+*   **Memory Bandwidth:** The dedicated memory system achieves data access rates of up to 640 MBytes/second (calculated as 40MHz $\times$ 8 neighbors $\times$ 2 Bytes/voxel) [S1].
+*   **Complexity:** In software, geometry operations, perspective, and gradient calculations consume approximately 80% of the computation time per projection [S1]. Hardware offloading eliminates this bottleneck.
+*   **Limitations:** The maximal resampled dataset size is limited to $512^3$ by the gradient buffer size [S1]. Original dataset size is limited by available memory (4Mbit ORAMs or 16Mbit DRAMs) [S1].
+*   **Precision:** All calculations for density and gradient values are performed in 16-bit precision [S1].
 
 ## Background
-This method is part of the VIRIM (Virtual Reality in Medicine) architecture, developed at the University of Mannheim [S1, S2]. It was designed to address the computational bottlenecks of volume rendering by dedicating hardware to geometry operations, which are the most expensive part of the rendering pipeline in software implementations [S1]. The approach is closely associated with the Heidelberg Raytracer and the basic visualization algorithm of VIRIM [S1].
+This method originates from the VIRIM (Virtual Reality in Medicine) project, developed at the University of Mannheim [S1]. It was designed to address the computational intensity of volume rendering geometry operations, which dominate software-based implementations [S1]. The architecture was later commercially distributed by Volume Graphics GmbH . It represents a shift towards specialized hardware (ASIC/FPGA-like) for the resampling stage, preceding modern GPU-based approaches by utilizing dedicated DSPs for the subsequent shading stage [S1].
 
